  "content": {
    "id": "p66-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "$P(x)=2x^3+x^2-13x+6$. Evaluate $P(0.5)$ and hence find the zeroes of $P(x)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$P(0.5)=0$; zeroes: $x=\\frac12,-3,2$.",
      "worked": "$$P(0.5)=2(0.5)^3+(0.5)^2-13(0.5)+6=0.25+0.25-6.5+6=0.$$\nBy the factor theorem, $x-\\frac12$, and hence $2x-1$, is a factor.\nDivide $2x^3+x^2-13x+6$ by $2x-1$.\nThe first quotient term is $x^2$. Subtracting $x^2(2x-1)$ leaves $2x^2-13x+6$.\nThe next quotient term is $x$. Subtracting $x(2x-1)$ leaves $-12x+6$.\nThe final quotient term is $-6$. Subtracting $-6(2x-1)$ leaves remainder $0$.\nThus\n$$P(x)=(2x-1)(x^2+x-6)=(2x-1)(x+3)(x-2).$$\nBy the null factor law, the zeroes are $x=\\frac12,-3,2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 75
  },
